1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,990
Meet Nia.

2
00:00:01,990 --> 00:00:03,328
She's a site scout,

3
00:00:03,328 --> 00:00:05,840
and her new job sounds simple.

4
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:07,240
An AI company wants to build a

5
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:08,442
giant data center campus,

6
00:00:08,442 --> 00:00:09,508
and Nia has to find it a

7
00:00:09,508 --> 00:00:10,260
home.

8
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:12,860
Her boss gives her one rule.

9
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,190
Don't look for workers.

10
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:16,910
Look for the plug.

11
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:19,246
This place will drink as much power

12
00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:21,262
as a whole city,

13
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:24,490
every hour of every day.

14
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:25,935
A century ago,

15
00:00:25,935 --> 00:00:28,060
companies went hunting for oil.

16
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,150
Now Nia is hunting for cheap electricity.

17
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:34,413
So could cheap electricity become the new

18
00:00:34,413 --> 00:00:35,285
oil?

19
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:47,759
To keep her company,

20
00:00:47,759 --> 00:00:49,857
the team gave Nia a scale model

21
00:00:49,857 --> 00:00:51,360
of the campus.

22
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:53,050
They call it Cubby.

23
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:56,756
Cubby is a building that turns electricity

24
00:00:56,756 --> 00:00:58,074
into answers,

25
00:00:58,074 --> 00:00:59,510
and into heat.

26
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:00,093
Once it's built,

27
00:01:00,093 --> 00:01:02,040
it needs surprisingly few people.

28
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:04,139
What it needs is power,

29
00:01:04,139 --> 00:01:05,866
huge amounts of it,

30
00:01:05,866 --> 00:01:07,593
all day and all night,

31
00:01:07,593 --> 00:01:08,778
for years.

32
00:01:09,017 --> 00:01:10,537
Nia's boss texts her a checklist.

33
00:01:10,537 --> 00:01:12,527
Cheap power.

34
00:01:12,707 --> 00:01:13,867
Lots of it.

35
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:14,827
Ready soon.

36
00:01:14,827 --> 00:01:16,167
Cool enough.

37
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:19,697
Close enough to the customers.

38
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:22,247
And a government that says yes.

39
00:01:22,537 --> 00:01:23,920
Nia looks at the list,

40
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:25,457
then at Cubby.

41
00:01:25,637 --> 00:01:27,056
That's not a building site,

42
00:01:27,056 --> 00:01:27,927
she says.

43
00:01:27,927 --> 00:01:30,123
That's a treasure map.

44
00:01:30,383 --> 00:01:33,373
And Cubby has a lot of siblings.

45
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:36,519
The International Energy Agency projects

46
00:01:36,519 --> 00:01:38,417
that by the end of this decade,

47
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:40,550
the world's data centers could use about

48
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:43,110
as much electricity as all of Japan

49
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:44,563
uses today.

50
00:01:44,943 --> 00:01:47,342
That's a whole new industrial country,

51
00:01:47,342 --> 00:01:48,861
appearing on the map,

52
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:51,442
and it has to plug in somewhere.

53
00:01:51,683 --> 00:01:54,067
We've seen a map redrawn like this

54
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:55,263
before.

55
00:01:55,383 --> 00:01:57,529
In the twentieth century,

56
00:01:57,529 --> 00:02:00,761
oil decided where whole cities boomed,

57
00:02:00,761 --> 00:02:02,363
which ports got busy,

58
00:02:02,363 --> 00:02:04,593
and which countries got rich.

59
00:02:04,843 --> 00:02:07,500
Refineries rose beside the wells,

60
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,916
and tankers carried the rest of the

61
00:02:09,916 --> 00:02:11,053
world's share.

62
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:14,978
Nia wonders whether electricity is about

63
00:02:14,978 --> 00:02:16,961
to redraw that map.

64
00:02:17,217 --> 00:02:19,369
Let me draw why the plug matters

65
00:02:19,369 --> 00:02:20,507
so much.

66
00:02:20,647 --> 00:02:21,907
Here's oil.

67
00:02:21,907 --> 00:02:23,597
It's easy to move.

68
00:02:23,597 --> 00:02:25,018
Pump it into a tanker,

69
00:02:25,018 --> 00:02:26,877
and it crosses an ocean.

70
00:02:27,227 --> 00:02:29,027
Now here's electricity.

71
00:02:29,067 --> 00:02:30,286
To move it far,

72
00:02:30,286 --> 00:02:32,526
you need giant power lines that take

73
00:02:32,526 --> 00:02:33,496
years to build,

74
00:02:33,496 --> 00:02:36,087
and you lose some along the way.

75
00:02:36,317 --> 00:02:38,377
Nobody ships a waterfall.

76
00:02:38,637 --> 00:02:40,510
So when the energy can't come to

77
00:02:40,510 --> 00:02:41,529
the factory,

78
00:02:41,529 --> 00:02:44,210
the factory goes to the energy.

79
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:50,780
And this has happened before.

80
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:51,930
Meet aluminum,

81
00:02:51,930 --> 00:02:54,090
the metal in your soda can.

82
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,970
You can't just dig it up.

83
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:57,393
You have to tear it out of

84
00:02:57,393 --> 00:03:00,230
its ore with a massive electric current.

85
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:02,553
Making a single ton takes about as

86
00:03:02,553 --> 00:03:05,476
much electricity as an American home uses

87
00:03:05,476 --> 00:03:07,050
in more than a year.

88
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:09,509
That's why people call aluminum solid

89
00:03:09,509 --> 00:03:11,131
electricity.

90
00:03:11,383 --> 00:03:13,710
So the smelters went hunting for cheap

91
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:14,460
power,

92
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:16,353
exactly like Nia.

93
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:18,159
In the nineteen sixties,

94
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:20,218
Iceland built its first giant dam mainly

95
00:03:20,218 --> 00:03:23,083
to power its first aluminum smelter.

96
00:03:23,143 --> 00:03:24,588
Electricity in,

97
00:03:24,588 --> 00:03:25,405
metal out,

98
00:03:25,405 --> 00:03:26,843
ships to the world.

99
00:03:26,923 --> 00:03:29,300
Iceland's national power company still

100
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,082
sells most of its power to heavy

101
00:03:31,082 --> 00:03:32,276
industry.

102
00:03:32,517 --> 00:03:34,297
Or take Canada.

103
00:03:34,447 --> 00:03:35,956
It doesn't mine any of the ore

104
00:03:35,956 --> 00:03:37,837
aluminum comes from.

105
00:03:37,837 --> 00:03:39,404
Yet it's one of the world's big

106
00:03:39,404 --> 00:03:40,949
aluminum makers,

107
00:03:40,949 --> 00:03:42,964
with almost all of its smelters in

108
00:03:42,964 --> 00:03:43,614
Quebec,

109
00:03:43,614 --> 00:03:45,557
beside giant hydro dams.

110
00:03:45,747 --> 00:03:47,487
The ore sails in.

111
00:03:47,487 --> 00:03:49,257
The metal sails out.

112
00:03:49,257 --> 00:03:50,225
In a way,

113
00:03:50,225 --> 00:03:52,186
Quebec exports its rivers,

114
00:03:52,186 --> 00:03:54,275
one ingot at a time.

115
00:03:54,517 --> 00:03:56,547
Now here's the twist.

116
00:03:56,717 --> 00:03:58,850
A data center is a smelter for

117
00:03:58,850 --> 00:04:00,517
the digital age.

118
00:04:00,597 --> 00:04:02,337
Electricity goes in.

119
00:04:02,337 --> 00:04:03,957
Answers come out.

120
00:04:04,157 --> 00:04:06,219
And answers are even easier to ship

121
00:04:06,219 --> 00:04:07,437
than metal.

122
00:04:07,677 --> 00:04:09,698
They travel down a fiber cable,

123
00:04:09,698 --> 00:04:11,727
almost at the speed of light.

124
00:04:11,917 --> 00:04:14,493
So Nia starts where the smelters went

125
00:04:14,493 --> 00:04:15,415
first.

126
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:21,761
So Nia pins up a map of

127
00:04:21,761 --> 00:04:22,826
the cheapest,

128
00:04:22,826 --> 00:04:23,618
cleanest,

129
00:04:23,618 --> 00:04:25,502
biggest power on Earth,

130
00:04:25,502 --> 00:04:27,740
and books a lot of flights.

131
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:29,140
Six pins.

132
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:30,550
One suitcase.

133
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:33,210
And one very excited scale model in

134
00:04:33,210 --> 00:04:34,918
the seat beside her.

135
00:04:35,183 --> 00:04:36,133
First pin,

136
00:04:36,133 --> 00:04:38,043
northern Norway.

137
00:04:38,093 --> 00:04:40,828
Nearly all of Norway's electricity comes

138
00:04:40,828 --> 00:04:42,069
from falling water,

139
00:04:42,069 --> 00:04:43,541
and the far north has more of

140
00:04:43,541 --> 00:04:45,193
it than it can use.

141
00:04:45,333 --> 00:04:47,214
In the port town of Narvik,

142
00:04:47,214 --> 00:04:49,595
a new AI campus was announced as

143
00:04:49,595 --> 00:04:50,762
Stargate Norway,

144
00:04:50,762 --> 00:04:53,743
picked for hydropower and cool air.

145
00:04:53,893 --> 00:04:54,549
Since then,

146
00:04:54,549 --> 00:04:55,884
the tenant changed,

147
00:04:55,884 --> 00:04:58,973
and Microsoft became the big customer.

148
00:04:59,073 --> 00:05:01,043
The plug stayed the same.

149
00:05:01,073 --> 00:05:03,640
Norway's oil fortune is the story we

150
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,293
told in Why Norway Is So Rich.

151
00:05:06,363 --> 00:05:08,872
This is its other energy.

152
00:05:09,117 --> 00:05:10,123
Second pin,

153
00:05:10,123 --> 00:05:11,467
Iceland.

154
00:05:11,467 --> 00:05:13,798
Power from rivers and from the heat

155
00:05:13,798 --> 00:05:15,068
under the ground,

156
00:05:15,068 --> 00:05:17,399
and cold air that cools servers almost

157
00:05:17,399 --> 00:05:18,517
